sec.p:
We used piecewise Bayesian regression to fit the dynamics of ON- and OFF-cells during trials with noxious stimulation. We binned spikes using a 50 ms time bin, assuming that the total spike count for ON- or OFF-cells per bin ytON\begin{document}$y_{t}^{O N}$\end{document}was Poisson distributed with a variable rate λt, dependent on parameters θ, such that:
(1)
ytON∼Po(λtON) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} y_{t} \sim \operatorname{Po}\left(\lambda_{t}^{\mathrm{ON}}\right) \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2)
λtON={fresponse(θ,t),t<tswitchfrecovery(θ,t),t≥tswitch \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \lambda_{t}^{\mathrm{ON}}= \begin{cases}f_{\text {response }}(\theta, t), & t<t_{\text {switch }} \tag{2}\\ f_{\text {recovery }}(\theta, t), & t \geq t_{\text {switch }}\end{cases}$$\end{document}
For ON-cells, the response phase was modeled as an exponential rise 33. We compared single- and double-timescale recovery functions. The double exponential model consisted of:
λtON={rpre+kpree(t−ts)/τ1ift≤tsrpost+kfaste(t−ts)/τ2+kslowe(t−ts)/τ3ift>ts \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \lambda_{t}^{\mathrm{ON}}= \begin{cases}r_{\text {pre }}+k_{\text {pre }} e^{\left(t-t_{\mathrm{s}}\right) / \tau_{1}} & \text { if } t \leq t_{\mathrm{s}} \\ r_{\text {post }}+k_{\text {fast }} e^{\left(t-t_{\mathrm{s}}\right) / \tau_{2}}+k_{\text {slow }} e^{\left(t-t_{\mathrm{s}}\right) / \tau_{3}} & \text { if } t>t_{\mathrm{s}}\end{cases}$$\end{document}
with continuity enforced by a corrected constraint:
kslow=kpre+rpre−rpost−kfast \begin{document}$$\displaystyle k_{\text {slow }}=k_{\text {pre }}+r_{\text {pre }}-r_{\text {post }}-k_{\text {fast }}$$\end{document}
Priors were:
log⁡rpre,log⁡rpost∼N(μ=3.0,σ=0.5)log⁡kpre,∼N(μk=4.0,σk=0.1)log⁡kpost∼N(μk=2.5,σk=1.0)β1,β2∼log⁡Normal(μ=log⁡(0.1),σ=0.5)β3∼log⁡Normal(μ=log⁡(1/50),σ=0.5)ts∼U[−4,4]withτi=1/βi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \log r_{\text {pre }}, \log r_{\text {post }} & \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu=3.0, \sigma=0.5) \\ \log k_{\text {pre }}, & \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\mu_{k}=4.0, \sigma_{k}=0.1\right) \\ \log k_{\text {post }} & \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\mu_{k}=2.5, \sigma_{k}=1.0\right) \\ \beta_{1}, \beta_{2} & \sim \log \operatorname{Normal}(\mu=\log (0.1), \sigma=0.5) \\ \beta_{3} & \sim \log \operatorname{Normal}(\mu=\log (1 / 50), \sigma=0.5) \\ t_{s} & \sim \mathrm{U}[-4,4] \\ \text { with } \tau_{i} & =1 / \beta_{i}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
The single exponential model for ON-cells was identical except for removal of the kfast term.

For OFF-cells we compared a sigmoidal and a gaussian CDF function as the response function 33 and compared a double timescale recovery with an exponential + linear recovery function. We constrained the sigmoid to pass through the point (ts, u0), joining the left and right side of the model. The general sigmoid has four parameters, slope k, midpoint t*, lower asymptote a and upper asymptote b, such that:
fsig(t)=b+a−b1+e−k(t−t∗) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle f_{\text {sig }}(t)=b+\frac{a-b}{1+e^{-k\left(t-t^{*}\right)}}$$\end{document}
The Gaussian CDF had form of:
ferf(t)=koffset+kscale∗(1−erf(kslope(t−t∗))) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle f_{\text {erf }}(t)=k_{\text {offset }}+k_{\text {scale }} *\left(1-\operatorname{erf}\left(k_{\text {slope }}\left(t-t^{*}\right)\right)\right)$$\end{document}
where erf refers to the Gauss error function. For both the Gaussian CDF and the sigmoid, we chose t* such that f (ts) = u0.

The full model consisted of:


λtOFF={b+ferf/sig(t)ift≤tsu0+(c−u0)(1−e−(t−ts)/τ)+k3(t−ts)ift>ts
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \lambda_{t}^{\mathrm{OFF}}= \begin{cases}b+f_{\text {erf } / \text { sig }}(t) & \text { if } t \leq t_{\mathrm{s}} \\ u_{0}+\left(c-u_{0}\right)\left(1-e^{-\left(t-t_{s}\right) / \tau}\right)+k_{3}\left(t-t_{s}\right) & \text { if } t>t_{\mathrm{s}}\end{cases}$$\end{document}



For the double exponential case, the right hand side of the model (t > ts) was replaced with:


u0+(c−u0)(1−e−(t−ts)/τslow)+(d−u0)(1−e−(t−ts)/τfast)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle u_{0}+\left(c-u_{0}\right)\left(1-e^{-\left(t-t_{s}\right) / \tau_{\text {slow }}}\right)+\left(d-u_{0}\right)\left(1-e^{-\left(t-t_{s}\right) / \tau_{\text {fast }}}\right)$$\end{document}



Priors were set as:


koffset,kscale=HalfNormal(σ=10.0)kslope=HalfNormal(σ=4.0)b∼HalfNormal(σ=4.0)a,c∼b+HalfNormal(σ=4.0)k,k2∼N(μ=0.0,σ=1.0)k3∼HalfNormal(σ=1.0)u0∼U[a,b]ts∼U[−4.0,4.0]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} k_{\text {offset }}, k_{\text {scale }} & =\operatorname{HalfNormal}(\sigma=10.0) \\ k_{\text {slope }} & =\operatorname{HalfNormal}(\sigma=4.0) \\ b & \sim \operatorname{HalfNormal}(\sigma=4.0) \\ a, c & \sim b+\operatorname{HalfNormal}(\sigma=4.0) \\ k, k_{2} & \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu=0.0, \sigma=1.0) \\ k_{3} & \sim \operatorname{HalfNormal}(\sigma=1.0) \\ u_{0} & \sim \mathrm{U}[a, b] \\ t_{s} & \sim \mathrm{U}[-4.0,4.0]\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



and additional priors for the double exponential OFF-cell recovery d and k3 were defined identically to c and k2 for the linear + exponential case, respectively. Markov chain Monte Carlo sampling using PyMC was used to sample from the posterior, using 1000 tuning steps, 4 chains, and 1000 samples per chain.

Secondly, slow structure in ongoing firing was quantified using latent GP models applied to binned spike counts (5 s bins). For each neuron, we fitted a sparse variational GP (200 inducing points) with a constant mean and a periodic covariance function:
(3)
x(t)∼GP(c,k(t,t′)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x(t) \sim \mathcal{G} \mathcal{P}\left(c, k\left(t, t^{\prime}\right)\right), \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(4)
k(t,t′)=σ2exp⁡[−2sin2⁡(π|t−t′|/p)ℓper2], \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} k\left(t, t^{\prime}\right)=\sigma^{2} \exp \left[-\frac{2 \sin ^{2}\left(\pi\left|t-t^{\prime}\right| / p\right)}{\ell_{\mathrm{per}}^{2}}\right], \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where p denotes the oscillation period and ℓper the periodic length scale. A narrow Gaussian prior ℓper~ 𝒩(0.1, 0.1) was used to avoid over-smoothing in low-rate neurons. Kernel periods were initialized at 300 s, based on the period-scan analysis.

Spike count variability exhibited substantial over- and under-dispersion across cell classes. We evaluated a range of like-lihoods (Poisson, Conway–Maxwell–Poisson), kernels (periodic, spectral mixture), and link functions. A generalized Poisson model 69,70 consistently provided stable fits and accommodated the broad dispersion range of ON-, OFF-, and NEUTRAL-cells. The generalized Poisson likelihood was parameterized by a parameter θt and dispersion parameter α:
(6)
P(Yt=yt∣θt,α)=θt(θt+αyt)yt−1yt!exp⁡[−(θt+αyt)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P\left(Y_{t}=y_{t} \mid \theta_{t}, \alpha\right)=\frac{\theta_{t}\left(\theta_{t}+\alpha y_{t}\right)^{y_{t}-1}}{y_{t}!} \exp \left[-\left(\theta_{t}+\alpha y_{t}\right)\right] . \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
θt was related to the mean µt by:
(7)
μt=E[Y]=θt1−α,|α|<1. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mu_{t}=\mathbb{E}[Y]=\frac{\theta_{t}}{1-\alpha}, \quad|\alpha|<1 . \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We placed a prior α~ 𝒩 (0, 0.5) and computed θt = µt (1 −α) at each time point. A small contamination term (pnoise = 0.001) was added to account for rare outlier bins:
(8)
p(yt∣μt)=(1−pnoise)GPois(yt;μt,α)+pnoiseU[0,ymax]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} p\left(y_{t} \mid \mu_{t}\right)=\left(1-p_{\text {noise }}\right) \operatorname{GPois}\left(y_{t} ; \mu_{t}, \alpha\right) \tag{8}\\ +p_{\text {noise }} \mathrm{U}\left[0, y_{\max }\right] . \end{gather*}$$\end{document}

We first binned each spike train x and heart rate y using a 2 s bin size. We split the recording into 500 s segments with a 50% overlap, which gave each segment frequency resolution 1/T = 0.002 Hz. We demeaned each segment then used a multitaper spectral estimator with discrete prolate spheroidal sequences (DPSS) (standardized half bandwidth NW = 3.5, number of tapers K = 6) to calculate the coherence 71, using the formula:
Cxy(f)=|S¯xy(f)|2S¯xx(f)S¯yy(f) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle C_{x y}(f)=\frac{\left|\bar{S}_{x y}(f)\right|^{2}}{\bar{S}_{x x}(f) \bar{S}_{y y}(f)}$$\end{document}
Segments were then individually phase-randomized to build a surrogate coherence distribution, and used a max-statistic approach to control the Family-Wise Error Rate (FWER) across all frequencies. The significance threshold was set at α = 0.001.
